WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:20.765
My name is Jo Bauer, but readers of Little Women may remember me as Jo March.

2
00:00:23.287 --> 00:00:28.392
Yes, I married Professor Fritz Bauer, and we operate a school called Plumfield.

3
00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:35.502
It's a few miles outside the town where I lived with my sisters, Meg, Beth, and Amy.

4
00:00:37.083 --> 00:00:41.965
Poor Beth passed away, but my other sisters are married and leading happy lives.

5
00:00:43.666 --> 00:00:47.447
My younger sister, Amy, is an artist and married to Theodore Laurence.

6
00:00:49.168 --> 00:00:51.889
We call him Laurie because we've known him for years.

7
00:00:53.309 --> 00:00:58.592
When we were growing up, he lived next door to us, and his tutor, John Brookes, married my

8
00:00:58.732 --> 00:00:59.412
older sister.

9
00:01:01.352 --> 00:01:03.514
Meg and John have ten-year-old twins.

10
00:01:04.995 --> 00:01:10.699
Demi is a Plumfield student, and Daisy is staying with us because Meg recently had another baby.

11
00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:13.621
But that's enough about the past.

12
00:01:14.821 --> 00:01:18.624
It's time to meet the Little Men of Plumfield—and Daisy too!

13
00:01:23.187 --> 00:01:30.072
One rainy spring Saturday, a ragged 12-year-old boy arrived at the door of a large, comfortable house.

14
00:01:32.064 --> 00:01:34.386
He gave a timid rap with the brass knocker.

15
00:01:37.008 --> 00:01:41.291
Although he could hear children playing inside, no one came to the door.

16
00:01:43.132 --> 00:01:48.055
He knocked again, but when no one answered, he hesitantly pushed open the door.

17
00:01:49.596 --> 00:01:51.378
And what a sight greeted him!

18
00:01:52.699 --> 00:01:58.723
Two boys about his age were playing tag while two younger boys chased a yellow puppy and a

19
00:01:58.803 --> 00:01:59.323
white one.

20
00:02:02.248 --> 00:02:04.849
Another boy sat quietly reading on the stairs.

21
00:02:08.491 --> 00:02:12.152
hello!" A dark-haired girl looked up from her doll.

22
00:02:12.172 --> 00:02:14.913
"Are you a new boy?"

23
00:02:14.933 --> 00:02:19.575
"I don't know yet," he said shyly.

24
00:02:19.595 --> 00:02:23.437
"My name's Nat Blake, and I have a letter for Mrs.

25
00:02:23.497 --> 00:02:24.317
Bauer from Mr.

26
00:02:24.357 --> 00:02:25.198
Theodore Laurence.

27
00:02:26.738 --> 00:02:27.939
He said she'd take care of me."

28
00:02:27.959 --> 00:02:32.834
"Wait here, and I'll take Uncle Laurie's letter to Aunt Jo.

29
00:02:34.295 --> 00:02:36.536
I'm Daisy, and that's my brother, Demi."

30
00:02:37.897 --> 00:02:43.121
Daisy pointed to the boy reading on the stairs, oblivious to what was happening around him.

31
00:02:44.482 --> 00:02:47.744
Suddenly one of the tag players slid down the banister.

32
00:02:48.985 --> 00:02:54.649
Unable to stop, the boy landed with a crash that would have knocked anyone else unconscious.

33
00:02:56.570 --> 00:03:01.505
Nat forgot his shyness and ran over, expecting to find the boy half-dead.

34
00:03:03.105 --> 00:03:07.446
However, the boy simply blinked rapidly and looked up at Nat.

35
00:03:09.467 --> 00:03:10.707
"Have you come to join us?

36
00:03:10.727 --> 00:03:12.968
I'm Tommy Bangs."

37
00:03:14.288 --> 00:03:17.189
Tommy jumped up, as if he'd just remembered his manners.

38
00:03:17.209 --> 00:03:20.170
"Come play tag with Jack Ford and me!"

39
00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:27.872
"I should probably wait to see whether I'm staying," Nat mumbled, looking around.

40
00:03:29.868 --> 00:03:32.869
He saw a large schoolroom and a cozy parlor.

41
00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:37.490
A dining room had a table set for supper.

42
00:03:39.251 --> 00:03:44.092
As the smell of gingerbread filled the air, Nat's desire to stay grew.

43
00:03:45.672 --> 00:03:48.653
Daisy returned with the news that Nat was to stay.

44
00:03:49.754 --> 00:03:51.314
She led him to Jo's study.

45
00:03:51.334 --> 00:03:54.255
"I'm delighted to see you!"

46
00:03:55.635 --> 00:03:58.196
Jo smoothed the hair from Nat's forehead.

47
00:03:59.728 --> 00:04:01.149
"I hope you'll be happy here.

48
00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:04.271
Oh my, but your feet are wet!"

49
00:04:06.573 --> 00:04:09.395
Jo bustled around and found Nat some slippers.

50
00:04:11.897 --> 00:04:12.877
She smiled at him.

51
00:04:12.897 --> 00:04:17.280
"These are too big, but that'll keep you from running away."

52
00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:25.046
"I don't want to run away, ma'am—" Nat dissolved in a fit of coughing.

53
00:04:25.066 --> 00:04:28.388
"How long have you been sick?"

54
00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:30.601
Jo asked, looking concerned.

55
00:04:30.621 --> 00:04:36.742
"I caught a cold this winter and never recovered," Nat said when he could speak again.

56
00:04:36.762 --> 00:04:40.843
"Sit by the fire, and we'll get some medicine into you."

57
00:04:43.184 --> 00:04:47.385
Nat had just swallowed a spoonful of medicine when the bell rang for supper.

58
00:04:49.025 --> 00:04:54.327
Jo led him into the dining room, where he found a cheerful man sitting with six children.

59
00:04:54.347 --> 00:04:58.948
"Nat, this is my husband, Professor Bauer," Jo said.

60
00:04:58.968 --> 00:05:03.971
"And I think you've already met our two sons, Rob and Teddy.

61
00:05:05.851 --> 00:05:08.773
This is Jack, Tommy, Demi, and Daisy."

62
00:05:10.273 --> 00:05:13.795
Nat nodded shyly and took the empty seat next to Tommy.

63
00:05:17.457 --> 00:05:18.657
did you used to go to school?"

64
00:05:18.917 --> 00:05:19.658
Tommy asked.

65
00:05:19.678 --> 00:05:24.920
"I didn't," Nat said, and Tommy's eyes widened in surprise.

66
00:05:26.548 --> 00:05:30.570
"I worked on the city streets, playing the fiddle with my father and a man named Nicolo."

67
00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:33.752
"That sounds like fun!"

68
00:05:33.992 --> 00:05:34.713
Tommy said.

69
00:05:34.733 --> 00:05:38.154
"It wasn't—I never had enough to eat."

70
00:05:40.075 --> 00:05:42.297
Nat paused to take a bite of gingerbread.

71
00:05:42.317 --> 00:05:47.099
"After my father died and I got sick, Nicolo took away my fiddle."

72
00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:49.881
"I bet Mr.

73
00:05:49.961 --> 00:05:55.624
Bauer will let you play his old fiddle," Tommy said, making Nat's face brighten with pleasure.

74
00:05:56.792 --> 00:06:03.077
Sure enough, after supper Jo handed a violin to Nat, and he began playing softly.

75
00:06:04.879 --> 00:06:08.102
As his confidence grew, the other children stepped closer.

76
00:06:08.122 --> 00:06:11.324
"Hey, you're good!"

77
00:06:11.584 --> 00:06:12.285
Tommy said.

78
00:06:12.305 --> 00:06:15.748
"Play something we know, and we'll sing along."

79
00:06:19.751 --> 00:06:25.416
Soon everyone was singing, but as the last notes died away, Nat began to sob.

80
00:06:26.917 --> 00:06:30.379
"Nat, what is it?"

81
00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:31.901
Jo asked with alarm.

82
00:06:31.921 --> 00:06:40.567
"You're all so kind, and—" Nat coughed until he was breathless.

83
00:06:42.388 --> 00:06:44.049
Jo wrapped her arms around him.

84
00:06:45.451 --> 00:06:49.113
"You have a father and mother now, and this is your home.

85
00:06:50.474 --> 00:06:52.616
Don't think of those sad times anymore."

86
00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:56.401
Nat gulped and managed a smile.

87
00:06:58.661 --> 00:07:04.283
After a bath, Nat relaxed in front of the fire while Jo bathed Teddy and Rob.

88
00:07:05.924 --> 00:07:08.344
When a pillow fight broke out, Jo laughed.

89
00:07:12.465 --> 00:07:13.846
you going to punish those boys?"

90
00:07:14.246 --> 00:07:14.946
Nat asked.

91
00:07:14.966 --> 00:07:20.988
"Of course not," Jo said as Tommy ran into the room and tossed a pillow at her.

92
00:07:22.312 --> 00:07:23.893
She fired it right back at him.

93
00:07:23.913 --> 00:07:29.976
"Our boys do whatever they want on Saturdays, and that includes pillow fights."

94
00:07:29.996 --> 00:07:32.618
"What a nice school this is!"

95
00:07:33.498 --> 00:07:34.258
Nat sighed.

96
00:07:34.278 --> 00:07:43.463
"It's an odd one," Jo admitted, "but we don't believe in making children miserable with too many rules

97
00:07:43.523 --> 00:07:44.644
and too much studying."
